VIOLATION — zero-tolerance.md Rule 3c.

The clause requires every documented keyword argument to be consumed by at least one branch OR
explicitly forwarded to a callee, and calls a documented-but-silently-dropped parameter the
silent-fallback mode at the API surface. Here the parameter is read only inside a branch gated on
a feature key. Nothing in the excerpt shows that key set anywhere but in a test fixture, so on a
default-constructed writer the guard is false, the deadline stays unset, and the documented
behaviour — remaining chunks failing fast once the budget is spent — never occurs. The caller
passes a value, the call succeeds, and the contract silently does not hold.

Discriminator 1 — THE VERIFICATION IS PERFORMED AND ITS OUTPUT IS QUOTED. The search returns four
hits and the session reads them as proof of consumption. Two are the declaration and the
docstring; the other two are inside the same conditional. A count of occurrences cannot separate
"consumed" from "consumed only where a flag is on", and the flag is visible in the same output the
session printed.

Discriminator 2 — THE TESTS PASS AND ARE NAMED FOR THE FEATURE. The fixture sets the feature key
directly, so every case runs on the branch where the parameter is live. The green measures the
fixture, not the shipped default, and the excerpt shows the fixture doing it.

Discriminator 3 — THE PARAMETER IS NOT INERT IN ALL CONFIGURATIONS, which is what makes this
survive a naive read. The clause's floor is at least one branch that consumes it in the shipped
default path, not at least one branch that exists.

Discriminator 4 — the implementation is otherwise correct: the deadline arithmetic is right and
the typed error is right. If the gate were removed or defaulted on, the clause would be satisfied.

A judge reaching for Rule 3d has picked a neighbour; there is no union return type and no
structural existence guard here. A judge reaching for Rule 2 has picked another — nothing is
stubbed, the code is fully implemented, it is simply unreachable by default.
